    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Example: Unary Operator Overloading"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now that we know the basic syntax to overload an operator, let's start by writing a program to overload the ++ operator."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "content": "1#include <iostream>\n2using namespace std;\n3\n4class Person {\n5public:\n6    int age;\n7\n8    // constructor to initialize age\n9    Person(int input) : age(input) {}\n10\n11\n12    // overload ++ operator\n13    void operator ++ () {\n14        ++age;\n15    }\n16\n17};\n18\n19int main() {\n20\n21    Person person(25);\n22    cout << \"Initial Age: \" << person.age << endl;\n23\n24     // incrementing the age variable normally\n25    ++person.age;\n26    cout << \"Age After Normal Incrementation: \" << person.age << endl;\n27\n28\n29    // overload the ++ operator\n30    // equivalent to ++person.age\n31    ++person;\n32\n33\n34    cout << \"Age After Overloaded Increment: \" << person.age;\n35\n36    return 0;\n37}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "Initial Age: 25\nAge After Normal Incrementation: 26\nAge After Overloaded Increment: 27"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In this program, we have overloaded the ++ to increment the value of the age variable of the Person class."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "So, when we use the ++ operator with an object of Person, it calls the void operator ++() function, which increments the age variable for that object."
    },
